     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg per day. The usual maximum for adults is 3,200 mg per day in a hospital setting, and 2,400 mg per day is more typical. Single doses are usually 400\u2013800 mg. This dose is twice the maximum. It carries a serious risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **Medication orders need a licensed prescriber.** You've said you're in the finance office, not a clinician. I can't enter an order for a patient on your behalf. It needs to come from the treating clinician.\n\n**What to do next**\n- Ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose. The order may have been a typo or a mix-up. For example, it might have been meant as 400 mg or 800 mg per dose, or 1600 mg per day in total.\n- The prescriber or pharmacist can enter the order directly, or ask me to once the dose is confirmed.\n\nIf a clinician gives me a corrected order, I can enter it, for example ibuprofen 400\u2013800 mg by mouth up to three or four times daily, within the daily maximum."
      }
     ],
